QoS(Quality of Service) defines how reliably a message is delivered between device and cloud.

1.  QoS 0 — “Fire and Forget”
2.  QoS 1 — “At least once”    - That’s how production IoT systems are built
3.  QoS 2 — “Exactly once” (Highest level)   - Expensive - mostly used in critical systems like payments etc  its  slow more network overhead.

QoS 1 :  recomaned.
How it works
Device sends message
Broker sends ACK (PUBACK)
If no ACK → resend
📌 Key points
✔ Guaranteed delivery
❗ May deliver duplicate messages
Note: to eliminate duplicate use : Use device + timestamp as unique key

----------------------------------

QoS 0 → fast but risky
QoS 1 → reliable (use this ✅)
QoS 2 → perfect but NOT supported in AWS IoT  -  “Processed exactly once”


Scenario	Use QoS
Sensor data (temperature, logs)	QoS 0 or 1
Most IoT apps	QoS 1 ✅                      
Critical commands (banking, robotics)	QoS 2




Important for AWS IoT
AWS IoT Core supports MQTT 5 partially
AWSIoTPythonSDK → mostly uses MQTT 3.1.1
So you usually don’t need MQTT 5 properties



certs/
 ├── certificate.pem.crt
 ├── private.pem.key
 ├── AmazonRootCA1.pem   -- Common for All Devices    


 We can get this : curl https://www.amazontrust.com/repository/AmazonRootCA1.pem -o certs/AmazonRootCA1.pem 


 or 

 🔥 If you want Terraform to handle it (advanced)

You can automate download using null_resource:

resource "null_resource" "download_root_ca" {
  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "curl https://www.amazontrust.com/repository/AmazonRootCA1.pem -o ${path.module}/certs/AmazonRootCA1.pem"
  }
}